  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "London’s abandoned docks gave Sir Nigel Broackes a public regeneration task as the first chairman of the London Docklands Development Corporation. He worked with deputy chairman Bob Mellish and chief executive Reg Ward to prepare an area scarred by industrial decline for new uses. The corporation acquired land from owners including the Port of London Authority and British Gas.\n\nIts work began with practical obstacles: sites needed roads, drains and sewers before private businesses would invest. Public funding paid for that groundwork, making previously derelict land available for development. Broackes’s contribution to the corporation was recognised with a knighthood in 1984.",
    "html": "<p>London’s abandoned docks gave Sir Nigel Broackes a public regeneration task as the first chairman of the London Docklands Development Corporation. He worked with deputy chairman Bob Mellish and chief executive Reg Ward to prepare an area scarred by industrial decline for new uses. The corporation acquired land from owners including the Port of London Authority and British Gas.</p>\r\n<p>Its work began with practical obstacles: sites needed roads, drains and sewers before private businesses would invest. Public funding paid for that groundwork, making previously derelict land available for development. Broackes’s contribution to the corporation was recognised with a knighthood in 1984.</p>\r\n",
